que called  cross validation cross validation and  oftentimes what I'll do because I might  only have a limited amount of data and  this might be such a small test set that  I don't have enough information here to  be statistically to know statistically  how well I'm doing and so what I might  do is take my data and randomly  reshuffle it and take 80% for training  and 20% for testing  I might randomly reshuffle it again and  do it again and again and again and I  might average over a hundred times of  this random reshuffling training and  testing and so that's called k-fold  cross-validation so kay fold just means  how many times I randomly reshuffle my  data and use some for training and some  for testing okay and this is very useful  for certain types of tasks if I have a  task that's kind of interpol Ettore in  nature if i'm interpolating my training  data if what is in the future or in my  test set is an interpolation some some  kind of non-linear function of what's in  my training data i can probably build a  really good model using this  cross-validation framework but if i need  to extrapolate into the future that's  actually really hard to do in general  with machine learning so I like to think  about let's draw a little picture and do  an example if I have a cannonball  trajectory and so it's this parabolic  trajectory and I train my machine  learning algorithm on this blue region  it's actually really hard to develop a  machine learning algorithm that will  correctly extrapolate the behavior of  that ballistic ballistic trajectory okay  so this is something that's an example  that's not an interpolation where you  really need to generalize and understand  the principle of what's going on but in  cases where you just need to interpolate  Interpol Ettore problems image  classification audio voice recognition  ten  to be in that category if I have enough  data enough training data then most  problems can be made interpol Ettore or  a lot of them can at least 